A||||19||ernst||Should I buy a fiero?||||
Z||000000||ernst||09-05-99||01:12 PM||||I have the chance to buy a fiero with only 56k on it for a first car.  It's in great condition and I can get it for under twogrand.  Should I?  It'll be my first car, and I don't know much about mechanics.  I heard they are high maintenance...what's your opinion??<br>
Z||000001||Cooter||09-05-99||01:55 PM||||Fieros are really great cars, but keep in mind that the newest Fiero is over 10 years old. Even one with low mileage will suffer from the "old car blues"- rubber suspension components, brake seals, weatherstriping etc. .  will all suffer from age. I think that the price is good, but the car will need some TLC to keep it on the road. The good news is that the Fiero shares a lot of its parts with other GM vehicles, so replacement pieces are not too difficult to come by. If you buy the car, you will need a good source of information. Since you are here, this is the best place to come back to for answers. In the end, YOU must decide if the Fiero is right for you. I dont use the Fiero as a primary vehicle, even though mine only has 60,000 miles on it. I keep it more as a toy and use a more economical car for daily driving.(the Honda gets 40 MPG compared to the Fiero's 30 MPG- still not bad!)
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||09-05-99||03:49 PM||||Should you buy a Fiero? If you drive it, and like it... yes. I have had 2 Fieros and can't imagine walking outside and not seeing one there. I do know one thing, if you do buy this Fiero, in a few months you will be asking "Should I buy a second Fiero?"
Z||000003||CUTTER||09-05-99||04:27 PM||||I had my 87GT for almost 4 months now and love it. I did put a few hundred $'s in my car but it was all my fault. Like wore down the brakes till the disk fell off and stuff, anyways they are great cars and fun. Get the VIP off the car you want to buy and check it to make sure everything is a ok. It also depends on what year it is and if it was beat. I cant tell you if it is a good car or a bad car. If it seems good I would buy it....Its all a matter of chance...           Hope this helped
Z||000004||88 Fiero||09-05-99||05:09 PM||||If you are buying a 4 cylinder at least try to look for an '87 or '88 model reason for that is because the engine design is alot better than the old ones...plus you get a little more hp...these cars are good as long as you take good care of them...mid engine cars are not good for everyday commuting specially here in california where it gets really hot and humidity..they don't like it very much....anyway...my fiero is always garage and only driven once in a while or in club meets....
Z||000005||theogre||09-05-99||05:19 PM||||Fiero? not good for daily driver? NOT!<p>I've put 20,000+ miles on mine in the last year. It's got 136,000+ on it now. I drive it every day. Anywhere from 30-300 miles in one day........<p>If you are going to drive it allot there are a few things you can do that really make the car happy, but there is no reason not to have one as a daily driver.
Z||000006||88 Fiero||09-05-99||06:06 PM||||well...Ogre if you live in a state where you don't get stuck on traffic and it doesn't move an inch every half an hour go ahead and buy one...but here in california it's not practical...you have to understand that the engine is in the middle not in the front like a cavalier, camaro or a honda...the fiero engine doesn't get ventilated till it moves so heat builds up real easy....not like the front engine car where there'a alot of ventilation....have any of you guys compare your fiero that just came from a bad traffic...open the hood and feel the heat and compare to a front engine car that's on the same traffic condition...I will bet you that your fiero engine bay is very, very hot...it's so hot you could cook a hot dog over it....
Z||000007||88 Fiero||09-05-99||07:07 PM||||what I am trying to say is that if you have the patience and knowledge on taking good care of this car..go ahead and buy one...but if you are thinking that after buying this car and maintaining it -- is cheap...then this is not the car for you...read some of the people who owns fiero's... most of them treat this cars are their toys and have another car for commuting everyday at least I do... and if they do drive it everyday...they know what they are up too if something goes wrong... also, like Cooter said this cars are over ten years old...your better off buying an import car if you are trying to be conservative at least you know it's very reliable in everyday driving...I'm not trying to scare the hell out of Ernst...but be practical about how you want you car to be treated everyday...The question is that if something goes wrong with his Fiero...can he afford to get it it fix without being frustated on the price of the parts as well as the labor.....
Z||000008||four50four||09-05-99||07:09 PM||||hmmm... I  thought the fiero was orginally advertised as a commuter car...
Z||000009||Patrick||09-05-99||07:15 PM||||<br>... maybe just in a <b>northern</b> climate...<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p><p>
Z||000010||lowCG||09-06-99||12:45 AM||||The only thing that got to me after driving an hour each way to,and from work every day,was the getting in,and out of that low car.It takes a technique using your hand on the pillar to lift yourself,in order to save your back.<br>A small price to pay though,really,and was a very reliable,and comfortable car indeed.
Z||000011||RFMike||09-06-99||06:04 AM||||I have to agree with Ogre.  My Fiero is my daily driver, and I drive 30-35,000 miles a year, 85-350 miles a day.  I'm from Wisconsin, and contrary to popular belief, it gets hotter than hell here.  I get stuck in snail pace traffic, and have never had an overheating problem.  My fan relay works.<br>Yes, there are things to fix.  Yes, if you can't do it yourself, it will cost you some dough.  The most difficult aspect of repairs and maintainence is diagnosing the problem.  There are a number of people at this site with a lot of experience, who are more than willing to share (thanks, guys).  If you are willing to learn, you can.  With some on-line help and a Haynes manual, a person of average intelligence and above average patience and persistence could do a lot of the maintainence himself.<br>What some of you are missing is the fact that he wants a car for $2,000.  For that kind of money, Ernst will be getting an older used car of some kind, with related maintaince.  Ernst, if you like to drive, the Fiero is for you.
Z||000012||theogre||09-06-99||06:21 AM||||no stop and go here? you ever tried to get thru I76, or I476, around Philly durring a rushhour? It's not uncommon for it to take over an hour to "do the blue route" which is only about 16 miles. Add the weather we've had this summer......<p>I've driven allot of front wheel drive cars. I think you'll find many of them not as well ventilated as you think. Depending on what set of grates you have in the back deck of Fiero it's allot better at convection cooling than it would seem, add even a little moving air and it's fine. Even on 100 degree days here, with the 180 thermostat in it now, the block temp as reported be the ECM is never above 195. Unless I shut off the fan radiator fan. With the 195 stat it sat right about 215 regaurdless of driving conditions.
Z||000013||88 Fiero||09-06-99||07:41 AM||||hey Ernst...I hope you would not think that I don't want you to buy a fiero...just want to be sure that your not going to get stuck on something that you can't afford on repairs...co'z I guarrantee you that you will  need to replace a part on that car after you buy it...anyway ....good luck!!!
Z||000014||ChadMan||09-07-1999||08:06 PM||||My short answer is yes, Ernst, you should buy a Fiero.  They're cheap, they're fun, they're unique, and they never rust.  My daily driver has been a Fiero of one sort or another since 4/92.<P>Having said that, here are a few observations:<P>Later cars are more reliable than early cars, not just because they're newer, but because of significant improvements Pontiac made.<P>Manual tranny is more fun, but there are three kinds of Fiero clutches: broken, about to break, and just fixed.  If the Fiero will be your only car, seriously consider an automatic.<P>Both the V6 and the 4 are basically good motors.  V6 cars are harder to work on but will generally take more abuse than the 4 banger.<P>Make friends with someone who owns a pickup.  Rarely do I need more luggage space than the Fiero has, but when I do, I'm glad to know Ranger and Dakota drivers.||||reg||
Z||000015||ernst||09-07-1999||09:58 PM||||Yeah..I live in Rochester NY, so the weather won't be too hot for it.  It is an automatic 4'banger'.  Thanks for all the help guys.<BR>                         ~ERNST<BR>||||reg||
Z||000016||Cliff D.||09-08-1999||08:55 PM||||Sounds like you will have more problems with snow and ice in winter than heat in summer. Probably the best race I ever had was me in my firebird and my buddy in his (mine now) fiero in 4 inches of snow. you can't tell me that 4-wheeling is more fun than spinning uncontrollably in a rear wheel drive car.||||reg||
Z||000017||batboy||09-11-1999||01:42 PM||||I have 3 Fieros, so I'm biased, but I say buy one if you find a good car. The one you described sounds pretty good. Shop around. They do require an owner to learn about the car and to work on them sometimes. Shops will charge more to work on Fieros because they are different and sometimes are more labor consuming to work on then other cars. But, they are a tough, fun to drive, great looking little vehicle. Maybe even soon to become a classic collectible!?!||||reg||1
Z||000018||Shiner||09-13-1999||01:05 AM||||I just bought an '87 GT 5speed to replace my '84 sport coupe 3speed auto.  The 4cyl/3speed is the SLOWEST fiero you can buy, especially the '84.  My mother's '88 Isuzu Trooper 5speed flys compared to that thing!  And that's saying alot.  In comparison, the GT absolutly cooks, ask for power, and you got it.  The 5speed transmission REALLY makes it much more fun.  Whatever you do, good luck||||reg||7
Z||000019||Standard||09-13-1999||01:35 AM||||the 4 cyl/3 speed auto may be the slowest fiero, but it's not a slow car... I beat plenty of people off of red lights.. then again, maybe if I let them know i was racing them it would turn out different... oh well  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
